* Operational amplifier models written for this project. None of these is a
* model of a manufacturer. Every figure is from the datasheet named beside
* it; the qualification benches of the block "models" compare each model
* with those figures.

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* Unit noise sources for one amplifier: the open-circuit voltage of
* resistors, because a behavioral source makes no noise.
*   nw   white, 1 nV/rtHz: one resistor of (1 nV)^2 / 4kT = 60.328 ohm at
*        27 C.
*   nf   1/f, 1 uV/rtHz at 1 Hz: eight resistors with equal capacitors,
*        one corner per decade from 0.01 Hz to 100 kHz. The sum follows
*        1/f within 3 % of the density between 0.03 Hz and 100 kHz.
* An amplifier scales the two with the gain of a controlled source.
.subckt OPAMP_UNITNOISE nw nf
Rw nw 0 60.328
R1 nf n1 8.8434e9
C1 nf n1 1.7997n
R2 n1 n2 8.8434e8
C2 n1 n2 1.7997n
R3 n2 n3 8.8434e7
C3 n2 n3 1.7997n
R4 n3 n4 8.8434e6
C4 n3 n4 1.7997n
R5 n4 n5 8.8434e5
C5 n4 n5 1.7997n
R6 n5 n6 8.8434e4
C6 n5 n6 1.7997n
R7 n6 n7 8.8434e3
C7 n6 n7 1.7997n
R8 n7 0 8.8434e2
C8 n7 0 1.7997n
.ends OPAMP_UNITNOISE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* One macromodel for the voltage-feedback amplifiers of the board.
*   Pins: non-inverting input, inverting input, positive supply, negative
*   supply, output.
*   gbw    gain-bandwidth product, Hz
*   sr     slew rate, V/s
*   aol    open-loop gain at DC, V/V
*   f2     second pole, Hz (sets the phase margin at unity gain)
*   vos    input offset voltage, V (the output of a follower sits vos below
*          its input)
*   ib     input bias current, A (flows into both inputs when positive)
*   en     input voltage noise density, white part, V/rtHz
*   fc     frequency below which the 1/f part of the voltage noise is the
*          larger one, Hz
*   rout   open-loop output resistance, ohm
*   cout   capacitance across the open-loop output resistance, F
*   rsat   resistance of the output while it rests against a rail, ohm
*   vhi    distance the unloaded output keeps from the positive supply, V
*   vlo    distance the unloaded output keeps from the negative supply, V
*   isc    output current limit, A
*   cin    input capacitance to the negative supply, each input, F
*   cdif   capacitance between the two inputs, F
*   iq     supply current at a supply voltage of vsq, A
*   giq    change of the supply current with the supply voltage, A/V
*   vsq    supply voltage at which iq is stated, V
* What it does: one dominant pole, one second pole, slew limit, an output
* that follows the supply pins (against a rail it is a resistance to that
* rail), current limit, white and 1/f voltage noise, supply current with
* the output current taken from the supply pins. The output is dead while
* the supply is below 1.5 V.
* What it leaves out: current noise, common-mode rejection, supply
* rejection, input range limits, overload recovery time, the fall of the
* open-loop gain with the load, distortion, temperature.
* Supply present: 1 above 1.5 V between the supply pins, 0 below.
.func opamp_alive(vs) {0.5*(1 + tanh((vs - 1.5)/0.1))}
.subckt OPAMP_VFB inp inn vp vn out
+ params: gbw=10meg sr=20meg aol=1meg f2=30meg vos=0 ib=0 en=5n rout=50
+ vhi=0.05 vlo=0.05 isc=50m cin=3p fc=0 cout=0 rsat=50 cdif=0 iq=0 giq=0
+ vsq=5
* input: noise, offset, bias current, capacitance
Xn nw nf OPAMP_UNITNOISE
Enw inp ip0 nw 0 {en/1n}
Enf ip0 ip1 nf 0 {en*sqrt(fc)/1u}
Vos ip1 ip {vos}
Ibp ip vn {ib}
Ibn inn vn {ib}
Cip inp vn {cin}
Cin inn vn {cin}
Cid inp inn {cdif}
* transconductance into the integrator, limited to the slew rate
Bgm 0 x I = max(min({6.2832*gbw*1n}*v(ip,inn), {sr*1n}), {-sr*1n})
Cx x 0 1n
Rx x 0 {aol/(6.2832*gbw*1n)}
* the integrator may not run away while the output is against a rail: it
* stops a little beyond the rail, plus what the output current needs across
* the open-loop output resistance
Bwind x 0 I = 0.1*(max(v(x) - (v(vn) + 0.01 + opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*(v(vp,vn) - {vhi} - 0.01)) - 0.05 - {rout}*max(v(io), 0), 0) + min(v(x) - (v(vn) - 0.01 + opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*({vlo} + 0.01)) + 0.05 - {rout}*min(v(io), 0), 0))
* second pole
Ep2 y1 0 x 0 1
Rp2 y1 y 1k
Cp2 y 0 {1/(6.2832*f2*1k)}
Ey yb 0 y 0 1
* output: the open-loop resistance behind the internal voltage, bounded by
* the current limit and by what the stage can do near each rail. Without a
* supply the two bounds close to 10 mV around the negative supply pin, so
* the output rests there; written this way the bounds do not cross at the
* first iteration of an operating point, when every node is still at 0 V.
Bio io 0 V = max(min(min(v(yb,out)/{rout}, {isc}), ((v(vn) + 0.01 + opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*(v(vp,vn) - {vhi} - 0.01)) - v(out))/{rsat}), max({-isc}, ((v(vn) - 0.01 + opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*({vlo} + 0.01)) - v(out))/{rsat}))
Bout 0 out I = v(io)
Rleak yb out 1e6
Cout yb out {cout}
* supply current: the quiescent current and the current of the load
Bqp vp 0 I = opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*({iq} + {giq}*(v(vp,vn) - {vsq})) + max(v(io), 0)
Bqn 0 vn I = opamp_alive(v(vp,vn))*({iq} + {giq}*(v(vp,vn) - {vsq})) + max(-v(io), 0)
.ends OPAMP_VFB

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* OPA197 (U17, U25, U26). Texas Instruments datasheet SBOS737C of March
* 2018, for a supply of 8 V to 36 V, typical at 25 C unless a limit is
* named:
*   Unity-gain bandwidth 10 MHz, slew rate 20 V/us (page 8). Figure 10
*   (page 13) shows about 50 degrees of phase margin with 15 pF. The model
*   has a gain-bandwidth product of 11 MHz and a second pole at 14 MHz: the
*   gain then falls through 1 at 8 MHz with 55 degrees.
*   Open-loop gain 134 dB into 2 kohm, 120 dB at the least (page 7).
*   Offset 25 uV typical of either sign, 100 uV at most (page 7): the
*   parameter vos, 0 by default.
*   Bias current 5 pA typical, 20 pA at most at 25 C, 5 nA at most over
*   temperature (page 7): the parameter ib.
*   Voltage noise 5.5 nV/rtHz at 1 kHz, 10.5 nV/rtHz at 100 Hz and
*   1.3 uV peak to peak from 0.1 Hz to 10 Hz (page 7): 5.0 nV/rtHz white
*   with a corner of 340 Hz gives 5.8, 10.5 and 1.3.
*   Open-loop output impedance 375 ohm at 1 MHz (page 8); figure 26
*   (page 16) shows it flat from 100 Hz to 1 MHz and lower above. The
*   100 pF across it, the second pole and the product above are one fit to
*   table 3 (page 23), the isolation resistors for 45 degrees of margin
*   with 100 pF to 1 uF, and to figure 28 (page 16), the overshoot without
*   such a resistor: the model gives 44 to 55 degrees where the table says
*   45 to 47, and 51 and 28 degrees with 100 pF and 1 nF alone. The
*   resistors that the table gives for 60 degrees leave 70 to 89 degrees in
*   the model: no output impedance that is a resistance up to 1 MHz
*   reproduces that column.
*   Output swing from a rail 5 mV without load, 95 mV into 10 kohm and
*   430 mV into 2 kohm at +/-18 V (page 8): 5 mV and 48 ohm.
*   Short-circuit current 65 mA (page 8).
*   Input capacitance 6.4 pF to the supply at each input, 1.6 pF between
*   the inputs (page 7).
*   Supply current 1 mA, 1.3 mA at most (page 8).
*   Not in the model: the second input pair, which takes over within 3 V
*   of the positive supply with 12.5 nV/rtHz and another offset; the
*   current noise of 1.5 fA/rtHz.
.subckt OPA197 inp inn vp vn out params: vos=0 ib=5p
X1 inp inn vp vn out OPAMP_VFB gbw=11meg sr=20meg aol=5meg f2=14meg
+ vos={vos} ib={ib} en=5.0n fc=340 rout=375 cout=100p rsat=48 vhi=5m vlo=5m
+ isc=65m cin=6.4p cdif=1.6p iq=1m giq=0 vsq=16
.ends OPA197

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* OPA365 (U19, U28, U29). Texas Instruments datasheet SBOS365G of May
* 2023, typical at 25 C and 5 V unless a limit is named:
*   Gain-bandwidth product 50 MHz, slew rate 25 V/us (page 7).
*   Open-loop gain 120 dB, 100 dB at the least (page 6).
*   Second pole 70 MHz: fitted to the phase of figure 7-1 (page 8), about
*   -100 degrees at 10 MHz, and to the overshoot of figure 7-16 (page 10).
*   Offset 100 uV typical of either sign, 200 uV at most (page 6): the
*   parameter vos, 0 by default.
*   Bias current 0.2 pA typical, 10 pA at most at 25 C (page 6): the
*   parameter ib.
*   Voltage noise 4.5 nV/rtHz at 100 kHz and 5 uV peak to peak from 0.1 Hz
*   to 10 Hz (page 6): 4.36 nV/rtHz white with a corner of 6.5 kHz gives
*   both, and 12 nV/rtHz at 1 kHz where figure 7-14 (page 10) shows 13.
*   Open-loop output impedance 30 ohm at 1 MHz (page 7).
*   Output swing from a rail 10 mV into 10 kohm at 5.5 V, 20 mV at most
*   (page 7): 2 mV without load (assumption) and 29 ohm.
*   Short-circuit current 65 mA (page 7).
*   Input capacitance 2 pF to the supply at each input, 6 pF between the
*   inputs (page 6).
*   Supply current 4.6 mA at 5 V, 5 mA at most (page 7); figure 7-10
*   (page 9) shows 4.13 mA at 2.5 V and 4.28 mA at 3.3 V: 0.19 mA/V.
*   Not in the model: the current noise of 4 fA/rtHz.
.subckt OPA365 inp inn vp vn out params: vos=0 ib=0.2p
X1 inp inn vp vn out OPAMP_VFB gbw=50meg sr=25meg aol=1meg f2=70meg
+ vos={vos} ib={ib} en=4.36n fc=6.5k rout=30 cout=0 rsat=29 vhi=2m vlo=2m
+ isc=65m cin=2p cdif=6p iq=4.6m giq=0.19m vsq=5
.ends OPA365
